Open Robotic Industrialization for Developing Economies

A Theoretical Framework for the Robotic Development Dividend (RDD)

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Co-author: DeepSeek , chatGpt, Claude 

Version: 2.0 — September 2026

---

Abstract

This thesis proposes a formal theoretical framework for open robotic industrialization in developing economies. It introduces the Robotic Development Dividend (RDD) as a marginal quantity derived from a household production function, replacing the previous ratio-based definition with a testable threshold condition. The central research question is: at what level of robot capital does an additional robot generate more household wealth than it destroys in human labor income? We derive a closed-form threshold condition from a CES household production function with displacement effects, showing that the RDD is positive if and only if the elasticity of output with respect to robot capital exceeds the elasticity of labor displacement with respect to robot capital, weighted by the relative income shares of robots and labor. The framework is validated against empirical evidence from Colombia (displacement dominates) and Vietnam (productivity dominates), demonstrating that the threshold is context-dependent and determined by the elasticity of substitution between robots and labor. We propose an identification strategy based on staggered rollout and instrument-based exposure to robot access, addressing the selection bias inherent in pilot studies. The technical architecture — Hardware → Open Control → Open AI → Local Data → Local Engineering → Local Manufacturing — is grounded in verified data on open-weight models (NVIDIA GR00T N1.7, Perceptron Isaac 0.5, Xiaomi MiMo-Embodied) and open-source control infrastructure (ROS 2). The Africa-specific strategy is reconstructed using independently verified market data, correcting the previously inflated figures.

