4. Technical Architecture: The Open Brain

4.1. The Four-Layer Stack

The robot stack is decomposed into four layers:

\text{Robot} = \text{Hardware} + \text{Control} + \text{AI} + \text{Production System}

Layer 1 — Hardware. Import affordable components: Chinese cobots with 5 kg payload cost between $12,000 and $22,000, compared to $30,000–$45,000 for Western equivalents, a savings of 40–60 percent. The Chinese domestic supply chain, anchored by harmonic reducer manufacturers that broke the Japanese price floor, enables this cost advantage.

Layer 2 — Open Control. Use ROS 2 and ROS-Industrial as the open control infrastructure. Open-source distributions represent 55–65 percent of deployed instances in Africa, demonstrating that this layer is already accessible.

Layer 3 — Open AI. Integrate open-weight models (GR00T N1.7, Isaac 0.5, MiMo-Embodied) for vision, planning, control, and reasoning. These models can be fine-tuned locally on domain-specific data (agriculture, maintenance, industrial processes).

Layer 4 — Local Data and Engineering. Locally produced data are used to adapt models and train local engineers. This is the layer that transforms an imported robot into a local asset.

4.2. The Lateral Entry Strategy

A developing economy should not attempt to compete with China in full robot manufacturing. It should enter laterally in the value chain:

\text{Software} \rightarrow \text{Integration} \rightarrow \text{Maintenance} \rightarrow \text{Assembly} \rightarrow \text{Components} \rightarrow \text{Full Manufacturing}

This progression allows for the gradual reduction of strategic dependency (software, control, data) while accepting temporary industrial dependency (motors, reducers, batteries).

