5. Economic Model: RaaS, Cooperatives, and Financing

5.1. Robotics-as-a-Service

RaaS lowers the entry barrier by converting a $100,000 purchase into a monthly subscription of $2,000–$5,000, including hardware, maintenance, and support. The model is already operational in Africa: OpenArm 1 rentals start at $800 per month in Nairobi. The global RaaS market is projected to reach $8.4 billion by 2030, growing at 16.7 percent annually.

5.2. Cooperative Robotics

The cooperative model is particularly relevant for agriculture, where smallholders cannot individually finance a robot. A cooperative of 20 families can acquire a fleet of agricultural robots and reserve operating hours. The AI coordinates the fleet, and ownership can be cooperative. Hello Tractor's pay-as-you-go model, with 60-month loan terms and local after-sales support, demonstrates the viability of this approach.

5.3. Financing Mechanisms

· ROSCAs (tontines): Integrate existing collective savings structures as a financing mechanism for first robots.
· Blended finance: Combine public grants, concessional loans, and equity investment.
· Payment at harvest: Agricultural cooperatives pay for robotic services after selling production, reducing risk for smallholders.
· Lease-to-own: SMEs lease robots with the option to acquire them progressively.

