9. Empirical Validation Strategy

The validation of the RDD framework requires an identification strategy that addresses the selection bias inherent in pilot studies. Early-adopter cooperatives and SMEs are likely to be more capable, more connected, and more entrepreneurial, which would bias RDD estimates upward.

Proposed identification strategy:

1. Staggered rollout difference-in-differences. Deploy the intervention in phases across matched pairs of sites. The treatment group receives robots in phase 1; the control group receives them in phase 2. Both groups are observed before and after phase 1. The difference-in-differences estimator identifies the causal effect of robot access, assuming parallel trends in the absence of treatment.
2. Instrumental variables. Use distance to port or subsidy-eligibility cutoff as an instrument for robot access. Distance to port affects the cost of importing robots but is plausibly uncorrelated with unobserved household capability. A regression discontinuity design around the subsidy-eligibility threshold can identify the local average treatment effect for households near the cutoff.
3. Heterogeneity analysis. Estimate the RDD separately by sector, robot type, autonomy level, and ownership model. The theoretical framework predicts that the RDD is positive when \sigma > 1, which is more likely in sectors with repetitive, dangerous, or precision-intensive tasks. The empirical analysis should test this prediction directly by estimating \sigma from factor demand equations.
4. Panel data requirements. The validation requires household panel data with observations before and after robot adoption, covering income, expenditure, time use, and asset ownership. The Colombian and Vietnamese studies provide templates for the identification of robot exposure effects using administrative and survey data.

